Achondroplasia (Acute Musculoskeletal Complication)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Rhizomelic micromelia, Macrocephaly with frontal bossing, Trident hand

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-002
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of lumbar radicular pain causing sudden immobility in a patient with vulnerable skeletal anatomy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an acute loss of independent mobility and severe pain in a patient with known achondroplasia and baseline spinal stenosis. Urgent medical assessment was strictly necessary to rule out acute cauda equina syndrome or severe cervicomedullary compression, and to provide immediate pharmacological intervention to halt functional deterioration prior to daytime services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My back and legs suddenly went into severe spasm, and I cannot stand up or walk to the bathroom."
History of Present Illness: Patient with known achondroplasia presents with an acute flare of bilateral radicular lower limb pain and severe thoracolumbar mechanical pain. Timeline: Baseline chronic dull ache in the lumbar spine; progressed rapidly 6 hours ago to sharp, shooting pains down both posterior thighs; culminated in severe muscle spasms preventing transferring from bed. Key symptoms include pronounced lumbar lordosis pain, bilateral leg heaviness, and exacerbation of pain upon attempted extension of the spine.
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound, entirely dependent for transfers.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver strain; spouse unable to safely assist with manual lifting.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/92 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, in severe distress due to muscle spasms. Obvious disproportionate short stature with rhizomelic shortening.
Physical Exam: Macrocephaly with frontal bossing and trident hand configuration noted. Neurological exam of lower limbs reveals intact sensation to light touch and pinprick globally. Symmetrical bilateral deep tendon reflexes. Normal anal sphincter tone reported by patient; no urinary retention or incontinence. Significant paraspinal muscle spasm in the lumbar region. No clonus or upper motor neuron signs to suggest acute cervicomedullary compression.
Environmental Observations: Bed height is inappropriate for independent transfer given current functional decline.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against physical pillboxes. Currently taking PRN paracetamol without relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute mechanical back pain and radiculopathy secondary to achondroplasia-related spinal stenosis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Cauda Equina Syndrome - Ruled out due to absence of saddle anesthesia, normal bowel/bladder function, and bilateral intact lower limb reflexes.

Acute Vertebral Compression Fracture - Ruled out due to absence of focal midline bony tenderness and lack of direct trauma.

Acute Cervicomedullary Compression - Ruled out due to absence of upper motor neuron signs, hyperreflexia, or respiratory compromise.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, immobilizing thoracolumbar and radicular pain on a background of achondroplasia. Clinical assessment revealed severe paraspinal spasm and radicular symptoms but definitively lacked red flag signs of acute neurological compromise such as cauda equina syndrome or high spinal cord compression. The severity of the pain crisis rendered the patient entirely bedbound, placing an unsustainable burden on the caregiver and presenting a high risk for complications of immobility. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period was medically inappropriate; urgent after-hours intervention was required to establish clinical safety, exclude catastrophic neurological deterioration, and administer parenteral and oral analgesia to restore basic functional capacity.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ketorolac 30mg for acute analgesia. Prescribed Diazepam 2mg BD PRN for up to 3 days for severe muscle spasm.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Adopt a flexed, fetal position while resting in bed. Consequence: Immediate widening of the spinal canal, reducing nerve compression and providing rapid pain relief (negative reinforcement).
Action: Take prescribed muscle relaxants exactly 30 minutes before attempting to move to the commode. Consequence: Smoother, less painful transfers, granting a return of personal autonomy (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommend immediate rental of a bedside commode.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours for ongoing pain management and specialist orthopedic spine referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department via ambulance if there is any new onset of bowel or bladder incontinence, numbness in the groin/saddle area, or progressive weakness in the legs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending advanced imaging. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Acute mechanical spasms generally subside with targeted therapy over 3-5 days, but the underlying anatomical stenosis remains a chronic risk factor requiring specialist follow-up.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you suddenly lose control of your bladder or bowels, or if your legs become completely weak and unable to move.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The injected anti-inflammatory reduces acute tissue swelling, while the diazepam breaks the cycle of severe muscle spasms in the back.
Actionable Steps: Rest in a curled-up position to take pressure off your nerves and experience less shooting pain. Take the medication before attempting movement to ensure the task is achievable and pain-free.

